      <h2>Three Things to Know First</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The Bible is God's Word.</strong> It is inspired by God and is the final authority for what we believe and how we live (2 Timothy 3:16–17). Feelings, impressions, and experiences are tested by Scripture — never the other way around.</li>
        <li><strong>The Holy Spirit helps you understand it.</strong> Pray before you read (John 14:26; 16:13). The Spirit opens up the meaning of the text, and He never contradicts it.</li>
        <li><strong>Read for the plain meaning.</strong> First ask what a passage meant to the people who first heard it. Then ask what it means for you today.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to Start Reading</h2>
      <p>Don't start at Genesis and try to push straight through. Start with Jesus, then fill in the story.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/bible/41-mark">Mark</a> or <a href="/bible/43-john">John</a> — who Jesus is and what He did.</li>
        <li><a href="/bible/44-acts">Acts</a> — how the Holy Spirit launched the Church.</li>
        <li><a href="/bible/45-romans">Romans</a> or <a href="/bible/49-ephesians">Ephesians</a> — what salvation and new life in Christ mean.</li>
        <li><a href="/bible/01-genesis">Genesis</a> — where the big story begins.</li>
        <li>Alongside these, read one <a href="/bible/19-psalms">Psalm</a> or one chapter of <a href="/bible/20-proverbs">Proverbs</a> a day.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Before each book, read its page here for the background: who wrote it, to whom, and why.</p>

      <h2>A Simple Way to Read</h2>
      <p>Take one chapter (or less) at a time and walk through four steps:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Observe:</strong> What does it say? Who, what, where? What words repeat?</li>
        <li><strong>Interpret:</strong> What did it mean to the first readers? Read the surrounding chapter.</li>
        <li><strong>Apply:</strong> What does it call me to believe, do, or change?</li>
        <li><strong>Pray:</strong> Talk to God about what you just read.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How to Avoid Misreading the Bible</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Read the whole chapter, not just one verse.</strong> A verse pulled out of context can be made to say almost anything.</li>
        <li><strong>Know what kind of writing it is.</strong> History, poetry, prophecy, and letters each read differently. Every book page here lists its genre.</li>
        <li><strong>Stories describe; they don't always command.</strong> Look for what the Bible repeats and teaches directly, not every detail of every story.</li>
        <li><strong>Let clear passages explain unclear ones.</strong> Scripture never contradicts itself.</li>
        <li><strong>Read the Old Testament through Jesus.</strong> It all points to Him (Luke 24:27).</li>
        <li><strong>Don't build a belief on one hard-to-understand verse.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Don't open to a random verse for guidance.</strong> The Bible isn't a horoscope.</li>
        <li><strong>Read with others.</strong> Bring your questions to a pastor, a mentor, or a small group.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>About This Site</h2>
      <p>Bible Sidekick gives a bird's-eye view of each book so you can read it with context. It's written from a Pentecostal perspective, in line with the Assemblies of God <a href="https://ag.org/Beliefs/Statement-of-Fundamental-Truths" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Statement of Fundamental Truths</a>. It's a guide to reading the Bible — not a replacement for it.</p>
